{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Fifth plan review 2026-10-18 (BLOCK, 1 move), a substantive one, confirmed on origin/main 21dabcbb2c1:\n\n`list`'s `sort_states` (`src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py:57-73`) preserves input order when `--sort` is empty, and ties break ticket_id-ascending with missing values last. The old `session-logs` verb's `recent_session_logs_state` (`reads.py:541-549`) sorts `created_at` descending with a plain stable sort and no tiebreak. So the proposed `list --type=session_log --limit=N` returned the first N rows in input order, not the N newest, and even `--sort=-created` could differ from the library on ties, breaking `tests/interfaces/queries/test_recent_session_logs.py::test_library_cli_mcp_agree` (`lib == cli == mcp` row-for-row at `:99-101`).\n\nResolution written into the description: the mapping is `list --type=session_log --sort=-created --limit=N` (the hint always includes `--sort=-created --limit=5`); `--limit` applies after `sort_states`; and `recent_session_logs_state` drops its private sort and reuses `sort_states(logs, \"-created\")[:limit]`, so the library, MCP, and CLI share one ordering rule and the parity test retargets its CLI arm and stays exact. `--limit` without `--sort` is documented as first-N-in-input-order, not newest. New AC pins all of that, including a grep that `recent_session_logs_state` contains no private `.sort(`.\n\nBehavioural note for the implementer: the only observable change for library/MCP callers is the tiebreak on equal `created_at` (now ticket_id ascending instead of input order), which the release note should state."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1792375498087304860,"uuid":"ef7bc64a-c74a-4858-b936-7e8d6d757f0f"}